/* ============================================================
   Cigar Cafe — 国・産地別の英語本文（後半4か国＋フィリピン詳細）
   英語版でだけ読み込まれ、CIGAR_DATA の該当項目を英語文に置き換える。
   日本語版はこのファイルを読まない。
   元データ: data/data.js + data/countries_deep.js + data/philippines_deep.js
   ============================================================ */
(function () {
  if (typeof CIGAR_DATA === "undefined" || !CIGAR_DATA.countries) return;
  var EN = {
    /* regions/sections は元と同数・同順 */
    "Ecuador": {
      flavor: "Ecuador is famed less for finished cigars than as a great supplier of the wrapper leaf that clothes the world's premium marques. Although it sits on the equator, thick cloud covers the sky throughout the year — a 'cloud-grown' environment that yields thin, smooth, beautifully coloured and highly aromatic leaf. Even within Ecuador the character divides by seed: Connecticut-seed leaf is golden brown, creamy and nutty; Sumatra-seed leaf carries nuances of cinnamon, flowers and earth with a faint sweetness; and Habano-seed leaf is said to give a medium-to-full flavour suggesting pepper, spice, caramel and cedar.",
      climate: "The stage is the fertile volcanic soil of Los Ríos province (around Quevedo) and Guayas province in the Andean foothills; ash thrown out by the country's chains of active volcanoes is said to have built mineral-rich soil that works as a natural organic fertiliser. The defining feature is the thick cloud, present almost all year round, created where moist Pacific air meets the Andean terrain. Where other origins stretch shade cloth to block the sun artificially, in Ecuador the natural cloud layer diffuses the fierce equatorial sunlight evenly, so fine-veined, high-grade wrapper can be raised under soft light with no covering at all. This natural shading delivers top-quality leaf without the cost of erecting and managing shade cloth — the source of the country's competitiveness worldwide.",
      history: "Tobacco for premium cigars is said to have begun to be grown in earnest in Ecuador in the 1960s. With the US embargo on Cuba sending the industry searching the world for a substitute for Cuban wrapper, Cuban exiles fleeing the Castro government reportedly added tobacco to the traditional Los Ríos crops of bananas, cacao and coffee. In 1967 the local breeder José Aray is said to have crossed Cuban (Cubano) and Sumatra seed, laying the foundation of today's standard variety, 'Ecuador Sumatra'. Connecticut Shade seed was later brought in from the United States, establishing 'Ecuador Connecticut', which uses natural cloud in place of shade cloth. Habano (Cuban-seed) wrapper joined from the late 1990s onwards, and today the country holds a firm position as one of the world's leading dedicated wrapper producers.",
      trivia: "Ecuador is a rare origin known to the world less for any particular finished brand than for a growing method itself — 'cloud-grown' cultivation. The Habano-seed wrapper raised there is hailed as 'one of the world's finest wrappers', and within some twenty years of entering the trade the country had become a mainstay of the world's premium cigars. One industry article even called it the world's best after around seventeen years of cultivation. Yet while a band may name the origin, the name of the company that grew the leaf rarely reaches the consumer's eye.",
      regions: ["Quevedo", "Los Ríos province", "Guayas province"],
      sections: [
        { h: "Permanent equatorial cloud, a natural shading device",
          body: "What makes Ecuadorian wrapper unique is the 'natural shade' its geography provides. Moist Pacific air striking the flanks of the Andes forms thick cloud that covers the sky over the Los Ríos district almost all year round. The fierce equatorial sun is diffused by this cloud layer and is said to fall on the fields as the same soft light you would get under a cheesecloth tent.\n【The economics of needing no shade】In Connecticut and other premium wrapper origins, whole fields are covered with shade cloth to obtain thin leaf with unobtrusive veins, at great cost in materials and labour. In Ecuador the natural cloud does that work, so thin, fine-veined leaf grows with no covering (that is, with only sun-grown facilities), and the costs of erecting, striking and managing tents disappear. The economics and quality of this 'cloud-grown' method are said to be the greatest factor that pushed the country to the front rank of the world's wrapper suppliers." },
        { h: "The terroir around Quevedo, Los Ríos",
          body: "Cultivation centres on the area around Quevedo, the commercial hub of Los Ríos province, and the neighbouring Andean foothills of Guayas province. Oliva's farms are said to be spread across several districts, from near Quevedo to the lower slopes of Guayas, more than 160 km apart in a straight line.\n【Soil raised on volcanic ash】Ecuador is a volcanic country with dozens of active and dormant volcanoes — sources count them variously, 'about 47' or 'about 30 active' — but either way the erupted ash is said to have formed mineral-rich soil and worked as a natural organic fertiliser. Where the warm, humid subtropical climate, fertile ash-rich soil with a well-balanced pH and permanent cloud cover coincide, the terroir of Quevedo is credited with producing, reliably, wrapper leaf that is thin and elastic, even in its reddish-brown colour and high in aroma." },
        { h: "A tobacco industry born of the embargo",
          body: "Ecuador's wrapper industry grew out of external pressure: the US embargo on Cuba, in force from 1962. Cut off from Havana leaf, the American cigar industry scoured the world for a replacement wrapper, and the people who answered were Cuban growers who had fled the Castro government. They brought tobacco to Los Ríos — banana, cacao and coffee country — and are said to have run repeated trials with Cuban and Sumatra seed.\n【Aray's cross and the fixing of the variety】In 1967 the breeder José Aray is said to have crossed Cubano and Sumatra seed and created the basis of the standard variety now called 'Ecuador Sumatra' (accounts of how the variety was established differ, and it is hard to credit a single individual outright). Connecticut and Habano seed followed in turn, and by the end of the twentieth century Ecuador had fixed its character as 'the wrapper country'." },
        { h: "Ecuador Habano — the star wrapper of the modern era",
          body: "The wrapper in greatest demand in recent years is 'Ecuador Habano', from Habano (Cuban) seed. With Corojo falling away in the 1990s — its weakness to blue mould one cause — attention turned to Habano seed, which carries a Cuban cast of flavour, and commercial circulation is generally dated from around 2001.\n【The flavour】The leaf is thick and firm, rich in oils and supple, reddish brown in colour. It is said to give a medium-to-full flavour: an opening of pepper (black and white) and spice layered with caramel and brown sugar, cocoa and nutmeg, toasty cedar and dry earth. Depending on how fermentation and ageing are handled, its expression swings widely, from 'sweet and mild' to 'dark and powerful'. It fronts a string of highly rated cigars, among them La Flor Dominicana's Andalusian Bull (Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year for 2016, at 96 points) and Romeo by Romeo y Julieta (No. 3 in the same magazine's Top 25 for 2012)." },
        { h: "Ecuador Connecticut — the world standard in mild leaf",
          body: "'Ecuador Connecticut' is shade-seed tobacco bred in the Connecticut River Valley of the United States and grown in Ecuador. Golden brown — a touch darker than the US-grown original — it is said to give a refined flavour, creamy and nutty with leather, cedar and a hint of pepper, and it has become the staple of mild-to-medium, beginner-friendly cigars.\n【The leaf that filled the gap left by Connecticut shade】Shade growing in Connecticut itself began shrinking from the 1960s–70s under rising labour costs and the conversion of farmland to development, with harvested acreage said to have fallen to about 2,720 acres by 1979. Ecuador Connecticut, by contrast, is cheaper — the natural cloud makes shade equipment unnecessary — gives higher agricultural yields, and is said to need a shorter fermentation after curing; it has thus won the position of world standard, effectively supplying much of what is labelled 'Connecticut wrapper' today." },
        { h: "Ecuador Sumatra and Candela — the aromatic leaves",
          body: "'Ecuador Sumatra', raised from seed of Indonesian Sumatran origin, is held to be the country's oldest line, tracing back to Aray's cross of 1967. Light-to-medium brown, smooth and lightly veined, its virtue is a delicate, never overpowering flavour of cinnamon, flowers and earth with a faint sweetness on the finish, and many marques — E.P. Carrillo at their head — put it front and centre.\n【Candela, the green wrapper】Ecuador is also an origin for 'Candela' (double claro) wrapper, fixed green in colour. The vivid green comes from a special curing that dries the leaf rapidly at high heat while its chlorophyll is still intact, and the taste is said to be lightly sweet and grassy. Oliva and others are reported to have worked it alongside their Sumatra and Habano lines — emblematic of the breadth of Ecuador's varieties." },
        { h: "Why the world's premium marques lean on Ecuadorian wrapper",
          body: "In the modern New World premium cigar, Ecuadorian wrapper has become a de facto core material. The reasons are said to come down to three. First, shading by natural cloud means no shade cloth, so shade leaf of comparable quality can be supplied steadily at lower cost than from Connecticut itself. Second, the qualities demanded of a finished wrapper — evenness of colour, elasticity, good combustion — are all present at a high level. Third, breadth of range: one country covers the major wrapper styles across the strengths, Connecticut (mild), Sumatra (medium and aromatic) and Habano (rich and spicy).\n【Examples of use】Ashton VSG and Symmetry, Oliva Serie V, Arturo Fuente's Rosado Sun Grown, Rocky Patel Fifteenth Anniversary, Camacho, My Father, San Lotano and more — adopted widely, whatever the origin or house style." },
        { h: "Growing, curing and the growers",
          body: "Harvesting is said to be done by priming: the lower leaves are dropped first, then pickers work up the plant in stages over some twenty days. The picked leaf hangs in curing barns to dry for around 35–45 days, is then packed into 100-pound (about 45 kg) cases by type and harvest date, and undergoes a light fermentation called 'sweating' in heated warehouses. In Oliva's case the leaf is then said to travel to the company's own sorting and processing facility in Estelí, Nicaragua, for full fermentation and grading. Higher primings, fermented longer, are said to grow richer in flavour, and suitability is judged by the reddish-brown sheen and fine veins of the leaf as it comes out of the barn.\n【The two great growers】Cultivation is said to have been led by two houses: the Oliva Tobacco Company of Tampa (the Angel Oliva family — reportedly central to the introduction of Habano seed, with about 70% of its acreage given to Ecuador Habano) and A.S.P. Enterprises of Miami (the Perez family, renowned for its Connecticut-seed leaf)." },
        { h: "Industry and exports today, and the flavour in sum",
          body: "Ecuador is woven into the world trade less as an exporter of finished cigars than as an exporter of wrappers, and within some twenty years of entry it is said to have come to rank among the main exporting countries connected with premium cigars (assessments of its rank differ between sources). Because the growers' names never appear on the band, ordinary enthusiasts barely know them — yet one industry view holds that a considerable share of the cigars in any shop carry Ecuadorian leaf of some kind.\n【The flavour in sum】Broadly, the shared virtues of Ecuadorian wrapper are held to be a smoothness with little harshness, born of growing under cloud, and an even, handsome burn. On that base, Connecticut is sweet and creamy, Sumatra spicy and aromatic, Habano powerful with pepper and caramel — and this ability to draw such a range of styles within a single country is the greatest reason modern blenders prize it." }
      ]
    },
    "USA (Connecticut)": {
      flavor: "Connecticut Shade is a refined golden-brown leaf, thin and with barely visible veins. Its hallmark is a sweet, gentle, creamy flavour suggesting almonds and cashews, cedar, and blonde-roast coffee with cream — the very byword for mild cigars, widely loved by beginners. Connecticut Broadleaf, by contrast, is a thick, dark leaf raised in direct sunlight, giving rich, sweet-edged flavours of dark chocolate, espresso, earth, leather, molasses and baking spice; it is held to be the definitive maduro wrapper.",
      climate: "The Connecticut River Valley is blessed with flat terrain and fertile alluvial soil of glacier-borne silt, sand, loam and clay, and became a great producer of wrapper tobacco. Its temperate North American climate, however, makes the growing season shorter than in the Dominican Republic or Nicaragua, which is held to be one cause of its high costs. Before 1900 only Broadleaf was grown here, in direct sun; around that year, to counter the thin leaf of Indonesian Sumatra, growers in the Poquonock district of the town of Windsor began stretching cheesecloth tents over their fields to reproduce Sumatra's cloudy climate artificially. Softening the sunlight proved to yield thin, delicate leaf with unobtrusive veins, and this is said to have led to the birth of the 'Connecticut Shade' variety.",
      history: "Tobacco was already being grown in the Connecticut Valley by native peoples when settlers arrived in the 1630s, and farming is said to have begun in earnest around 1640 when farmers sent to Virginia for seed. By the mid-nineteenth century the stretch from Springfield (Massachusetts) to Hartford (Connecticut) had become a cash-crop belt known as 'Tobacco Valley', growing only Broadleaf at first. From 1900, when shade growing under cheesecloth began at Windsor and the Shade variety was born, Connecticut Shade established its standing as a world-class name in premium cigar wrapper. Acreage is said to have peaked at about 30,800 acres across the valley in 1921, then contracted over the long term under urbanisation, labour costs and the rise of the cigarette.",
      trivia: "Martin Luther King Jr., the future leader of the civil-rights movement, is said to have worked summers on the Cullman Brothers shade-tobacco farm at Simsbury in 1944 (aged 15) and 1947, earning his fees for Morehouse College. That first experience of the North, away from the segregated South, is said to have influenced the shaping of his thought. Labour-intensive shade growing also drew seasonal workers from small towns in the South, the Caribbean and Pennsylvania.",
      regions: ["Connecticut River Valley", "Windsor", "Around Hartford"],
      sections: [
        { h: "Geography and soil of the Connecticut River Valley",
          body: "The heartland of Connecticut Shade and Broadleaf is the floodplain along the Connecticut River, from around Springfield, Massachusetts in the north to around Hartford, Connecticut in the south — 'Tobacco Valley', as it is called, with Windsor and Poquonock held to be its centre.\n【Alluvial soil made by glaciers】The soil of this belt is silty, laid down from material carved out of the north in the ice ages; it contains sand, loam and clay, with an excellent balance of drainage and fertility. Together with the flat terrain, it is said to offer rare conditions suited to raising thin, uniform wrapper leaf.\n【A short season and the climate】The temperate climate brings strong summer sun but a growing season shorter than in the Caribbean origins. This climatic constraint is precisely what lies behind the region's later invention of shade growing — and its high production costs." },
        { h: "Cultivation from colonial times",
          body: "Tobacco growing in the Connecticut Valley is old. When settlers arrived in the 1630s, the native peoples are said to have been growing tobacco already.\n【Taking root as a cash crop】Around 1640, farmers sent to Virginia for seed and cultivation began in earnest; by the mid-nineteenth century the 'Tobacco Valley' from Springfield to Hartford was among the foremost cigar-tobacco districts in America. Demand is said to have surged during the Civil War.\n【The Broadleaf era】Before 1900, the only tobacco raised here was Broadleaf, in direct sun. Thick and powerful, the leaf went into cigar wrappers and binders, and it is the source of today's Broadleaf vogue. Records by LaMondia and others at the Connecticut Agricultural Experiment Station (CAES) likewise trace the long history of Broadleaf production." },
        { h: "The Sumatra threat and the birth of shade growing",
          body: "At the end of the nineteenth century, thin, beautiful wrapper leaf from Dutch Sumatra (now Indonesia) swept the world market, and Connecticut's growers watched their share drain away.\n【The failure of Sumatra seed】Farmers brought Sumatra seed home and tried to grow it, but under Connecticut's strong summer sun the leaves scorched, and the delicate leaf of cloudy Sumatra could not be had.\n【1900: the Windsor experiment】So the growers stretched tents of cheesecloth (cotton shade cloth) over their fields to block the direct sun and raise the humidity, imitating Sumatra's climate artificially. The first shade tent is said to have gone up in 1900 along River Road in the Poquonock district of Windsor. After trials with dozens of Cuban and Sumatran samples came the 'Connecticut Shade' variety — thin, pliable, its veins unobtrusive, finishing golden brown and oily after curing and ageing." },
        { h: "The technique of shade growing and its steep economics",
          body: "Shade growing demands as much labour and cost as any cigar tobacco. Wooden posts are driven in a grid, wire strung across the top carries the cloth, and roof and sides are covered to create a kind of vast greenhouse. Cotton cheesecloth was once used; today durable synthetics such as nylon mesh also serve.\n【Priming】Harvest works from the bottom of the plant upwards, three leaves at a time every 7–10 days, usually in five or six passes for 15–18 leaves, all picked by hand. The leaves are strung up during growth, picked, and sewn together — passing through human hands again and again, so the labour intensity is extreme.\n【One of the world's costliest crops】Top-grade shade leaf is said to trade at $45–55 or more a pound, and there are estimates putting farm establishment and upkeep at $30,000–40,000 an acre and a newly built curing barn at $100,000–230,000 (accounts vary)." },
        { h: "Connecticut Broadleaf",
          body: "Broadleaf, grown here since the 1600s, is the very opposite of Shade. It is raised without tents in direct sunlight, with the plants set wide, about 24 inches apart (in contrast, it is said, to the roughly 12 inches of shade tobacco, planted close so the plants help shade one another).\n【A thick, dark leaf】Bathed in strong sun, the leaf grows broad, fleshy and rich in oils, and through curing and ageing it turns a deep brown close to black. This colour is exactly what gives the maduro cigar its 'nearly black' look.\n【Stalk-cutting and long fermentation】It is traditionally harvested by stalk-cutting — felling the whole plant — and a method of drying it still on the stalk, so that nutrients pass into the leaves, is also used. Thick and oily, the leaf is said to need roughly twice as long as Shade to ferment and age, yet for all the work its price is also said to be modest, around $12 a pound." },
        { h: "Shade and Broadleaf: flavour and use compared",
          body: "Though both carry the name of Connecticut, Shade and Broadleaf stand at opposite poles in taste.\n【Shade — creamy and gentle】Shade wrapper gives a sweet, smooth, mild flavour suggesting almonds and cashew nuts, cedar, blonde-roast coffee with cream, and faint bread and pepper. Thin, pliable and handsome in colour and sheen, it is held to be the standard clothing for mild premium cigars.\n【Broadleaf — rich and sweet】Broadleaf (mostly as maduro) brings dense, powerful flavours — dark chocolate, espresso, earth, leather, molasses, baking spice, dark cherry — with the natural sweetness that only well-fermented, well-aged leaf carries. Its thickness is also said to slow the smoke. Their different uses make the two the contrasting twin flagships of a single origin." },
        { h: "The major marques that use Connecticut leaf",
          body: "Even now that Connecticut itself has shrunk as a growing region, Connecticut wrapper remains a favourite of the world's premium cigar brands.\n【Marques using Shade】Macanudo (General Cigar; Macanudo Café its signature), Ashton Classic (the core line of a brand born in the early 1980s), Davidoff and the non-Cuban Montecristo all trade on creamy Connecticut Shade (or equivalent substitute leaf). Diamond Crown, the top line of J.C. Newman, is said to have used Connecticut Shade aged for five years.\n【Marques using Broadleaf】Drew Estate's Liga Privada No. 9 wears a dark wrapper of Connecticut Valley Broadleaf, stalk-cut and stalk-cured, and carries the nickname 'Broadleaf Brie'. Its sister marque T52 uses a stalk-cut, sun-grown Habano-seed wrapper from the same valley and is said to show a profile with more pepper and oak." },
        { h: "Decline, and the work of preservation",
          body: "Connecticut's tobacco growing contracted dramatically through the twentieth century. The roughly 30,800 acres planted across the valley in 1921 kept falling under urbanisation, soaring labour costs and changing smoking habits.\n【The collapse of Shade】The shrinkage of the labour-hungry Shade crop has been especially sharp: as of 2025 only about 35 acres of shade growing are said to remain, all of it on a single farm in Massachusetts (per the English-language Wikipedia and other sources). Many growers are reported to have gone out of business or switched to the less demanding Broadleaf. 'Ecuador Connecticut' — the same seed raised in Ecuador's cloud-grown environment — has become the cost-driven substitute supply for the world's cigar makers.\n【Keeping the memory】The Connecticut Valley Tobacco Historical Society was founded in 1988, and the Luddy/Taylor Tobacco Museum in Windsor's Northwest Park keeps the fading tobacco culture in view today." }
      ]
    },
    "Brazil": {
      flavor: "Mata Fina is medium-bodied and smooth, built on natural sweetness, gentle spice and earth, with aromas suggesting cocoa, leather and cedar. Mata Norte is stronger and earthier, with coffee-bean and nut aromas to the fore. Arapiraca leaf, from Alagoas state in the north-east, is dark, with a sweet, spicy personality suggesting chocolate, coffee and pepper. Taken together, a distinctively sweet, full flavour and dark, oily leaf are held to be the identifying marks of the Brazilian cigar.",
      climate: "The traditional heart of cigar-tobacco growing is the Recôncavo Baiano, the fertile coastal basin around Salvador in the north-eastern state of Bahia, whose district-by-district microclimates yield leaves of different flavours. Mata Fina is sun-grown on sandy, clay-tinged soil in a hot, humid climate, harvested by stalk-cutting — felling the whole plant — and finished, after months of fermentation, into dark, oil-rich leaf. Mata Norte, in the drier north of the Recôncavo, is air-cured in the open and turns out stronger and earthier. Virginia and Burley leaf for cigarettes, by contrast, is grown by family farms in the hills of the three southern states of Rio Grande do Sul, Santa Catarina and Paraná — the two worlds differ greatly in both origin and use.",
      history: "Brazil is one of the world's foremost tobacco producers and exporters, and leaf growing took root in the Recôncavo of Bahia from the sixteenth century. Mata Fina has long been rated among the world's finest raw materials for wrappers and binders; in the eighteenth century the leaf of Cachoeira was reportedly styled 'the world's best' and prized even by rulers in China and Africa. In the modern era the German immigrants Dannemann and Suerdieck built the Bahian cigar industry, and in the late twentieth century the Menendez family, Cuban exiles, joined hands with the Brazilian leaf merchant Amerino to create Dona Flor. CAO, through the success of 'CAO Brazilia', is counted among the pioneers that made Brazilian tobacco widely known to the world's enthusiast market.",
      trivia: "Brazilian tobacco is prized less as a solo lead than as an 'accent' in blends, thanks to its unique sweetness and character, and it goes into the binders and fillers of many popular marques. On the other hand there are also 'Brazilian puros' made entirely of Mata Fina or Arapiraca, rated for putting that dark, sweet personality front and centre.",
      regions: ["Bahia", "Mata Fina", "Mata Norte", "Arapiraca", "Recôncavo"],
      sections: [
        { h: "Geography, climate and soil of the Recôncavo, Bahia",
          body: "The Recôncavo Baiano, centre of cigar-tobacco growing, is a crescent-shaped coastal basin surrounding the state capital Salvador, its towns — Cachoeira, São Félix, Cruz das Almas, São Gonçalo dos Campos — strung along the basin of the Paraguaçu river. Hot and humid throughout the year, with sandy, clay-tinged soils, it is said to raise leaf of a wholly different character from the growing districts of the south.\n【The microclimates of Mata Fina and Mata Norte】The name 'Mata Fina' ('fine forest') comes from the name of the zone, in the heart of the Recôncavo. The drier belt further north is 'Mata Norte'; even within the same Recôncavo, rainfall and dryness differ, and that is what makes the difference in the leaf. Some point to the organic matter that neighbouring cacao plantations work into the soil as contributing to the distinctive sweetness, but firm backing is thin and accounts are said to vary." },
        { h: "Brazil's tobacco history, colonial era to the present",
          body: "Tobacco reached the Recôncavo a little later than sugar, from the late sixteenth century onwards. Because it could be worked on smaller plots with fewer hands than sugar, poor Portuguese settlers could also take part, and a comparatively loose social structure is said to have grown up. Leaf tobacco served as a medium of exchange in the slave trade on the African coast, while the finest grades were exported to Europe. In 1674 a tobacco control board is reported to have been established, overseeing the rules for the Portuguese colonies as a whole.\n【Golden age and modern industry】In the eighteenth century the leaf of Cachoeira won world renown, and there were periods when it was held more profitable than sugar. From the late nineteenth century the German immigrants Geraldo Dannemann (founded at São Félix in 1873) and August Suerdieck built the Bahian cigar industry, which flourished on exports to Europe. In the late twentieth century the spread of the cigarette and competition from Central America are said to have cast a shadow over the old prosperity." },
        { h: "Mata Fina and Mata Norte — Bahia's two leaves",
          body: "Mata Fina is raised in the sun and harvested by stalk-cutting, and after months of fermentation becomes a dark, oil-rich leaf suggesting cocoa, coffee, leather and cedar. Medium-bodied and smooth, built on natural sweetness and gentle spice, it has traditionally been rated highly as both wrapper and binder.\n【The character of Mata Norte】Mata Norte is air-cured in the open in the drier climate of the northern Recôncavo, finishing stronger and earthier, with flavours suggesting coffee beans and nuts; for that reason it is said to serve mainly as filler. Products of the same Recôncavo, the two are a study in contrast, divided in use and taste by growing environment and curing method — and they are often combined to conjure the sweetness and body that mark Brazil out." },
        { h: "The leaf of Arapiraca",
          body: "Arapiraca is a city in the north-eastern state of Alagoas, and at the same time the name of the dark leaf grown around it. Called 'Brazil's tobacco capital', the district owes its thick, oil-laden leaf, it is said, to strong sunshine and sandy-clay soil. Sun-grown, the leaf reaches a maduro-like colour from deep brown to near jet black, and is noted for leaving white ash.\n【Flavour and use】It carries a sweet, spicy flavour suggesting chocolate, coffee and pepper, placed medium to full in body. During fermentation the pilón — the stacked mound of leaf — is turned over repeatedly until it reaches about 46–52°C (115–125°F), then rested for six months to a year to knit the sweetness together, it is said. Today it is used almost exclusively as wrapper, and in recent years its character has been winning fresh appreciation." },
        { h: "The distinctive fermentation behind the dark, sweet flavour",
          body: "The dark colour and sweetness of Brazil's cigar leaf are said to owe much to the long fermentation that follows sun growing and stalk-cutting. Mata Fina ferments inside great mounds of leaf called pilones — for as long as three years, by some accounts. In the process the leaf turns from light brown to deep brown; the sugars are drawn out while tannins and other astringent elements soften, and mellowness grows — so it is explained.\n【An affinity with maduro and oscuro】Leaf finished this way is rich in oils and considered well suited to maduro and oscuro wrappers. That the hot, humid climate itself encourages natural fermentation is another decisive difference from the cigarette leaf of the south. The result is that sweet, dark flavours — cocoa, chocolate, earth, leather — have become the common language of Brazilian leaf." },
        { h: "Wrapper, binder and filler for the world",
          body: "Brazilian leaf, above all Mata Fina and Arapiraca, has gone into cigars the world over as an 'accent' that adds depth and sweetness to a blend, thanks to that dark, sweet personality. The usual division of labour is said to be Mata Fina mainly as wrapper and binder, Mata Norte mainly as filler.\n【An emblematic adoption】Combined with traditional Cuban- and Central American-line leaf, it is valued for lending sweetness and body to blends that might otherwise turn monotonous. CAO Brazilia matched Nicaraguan filler and binder with a dark Brazilian wrapper (said to be Arapiraca, though there are accounts naming Mata Fina) and became one of the emblematic hits after the cigar boom. Less the lead of a single origin than a fine supporting player carrying a blend's depth, it has spread throughout the world." },
        { h: "Major brands and makers",
          body: "The modern history of the Bahian cigar was built by German immigrants. Dannemann (founded 1873 at São Félix) flourished on exports to Europe with Mata Fina at its core, and today makes cigarillos and hand-rolled premiums under a Swiss-based group. Suerdieck, which at its mid-twentieth-century peak boasted a scale styled 'the largest in Latin America', is said to have ceased operating in 1999–2000.\n【Dona Flor and Menendez Amerino】Dona Flor was born of the partnership between the Menendez family — Cubans who had been involved with Montecristo and H. Upmann in pre-revolutionary Cuba — and the Brazilian leaf merchant Mário Amerino. The factory stands at São Gonçalo dos Campos in Bahia, and the marque's name is said to come from a novel by the writer Jorge Amado. Beyond these, CAO Brazilia, from the American house CAO, spread the name of Brazilian leaf to the world." },
        { h: "An industrial structure built on world-leading leaf exports",
          body: "Brazil has been reckoned the world's largest exporter of leaf tobacco since 1993, and recent reports put its exports at roughly 450,000–500,000 tonnes and US$2–3 billion a year; one report has it shipping about 455,000 tonnes worth about US$2.98 billion to 113 countries in 2024. What sustains these exports is not cigar-country Bahia but the Virginia (flue-cured) and Burley grown for cigarettes in the three southern states of Rio Grande do Sul, Santa Catarina and Paraná.\n【Integrated production and family farming】In the south, over a hundred thousand small family farms grow under a well-developed 'integrated production system': companies supply materials, expertise and finance, and buy the whole harvest. The growers' association Afubra represents the farmers' interests, and Santa Cruz do Sul is said to be called 'the tobacco capital of the world'." },
        { h: "The flavour in sum",
          body: "The overall tendency of the Brazilian cigar comes down to a sweet, full flavour and an earthy aroma, centred on the medium-bodied. Mata Fina carries the smooth natural sweetness, gentle spice and undertones of cocoa and cedar; Mata Norte and Arapiraca carry the stronger line of earth, coffee, chocolate, nuts and pepper.\n【Pairings and standing】Because the dark, sweet personality recalls coffee and cacao, it is held to pair well with sweet, dark drinks — espresso, dark chocolate, rum, port. Far from being uniformly full-bodied, most sit at a middle strength charged with sweetness and smoothness; as a 'sweet, mellow outsider', distinct from the premiums of Cuba and Central America, Brazil holds a place of its own among enthusiasts." }
      ]
    },
    "Cameroon": {
      flavor: "Aromas suggesting cinnamon, baking spice, cedar, cacao and nuts, layered with toast, a restrained buttery sweetness, and nuances of pepper and leather. Never overpowering, dignified and middle-of-the-road in body, it is treasured as an elegant wrapper that holds complexity and approachability together. The surface shows 'tooth' — fine grains of oil standing on the leaf — which is held to be one source of its flavour.",
      climate: "It is grown in the hot, humid moist-savanna belt of west-central Africa near the equator, stretching from eastern Cameroon (the plains around Batouri) into the neighbouring Central African Republic (CAR). The soil is fertile, needing scarcely any fertiliser, and the temperature and humidity are said to suit tobacco. The leaf has a thin, delicate structure and does not take heavy fermentation, but that delicacy and the fine sheen of its oils are what generations of cigar makers have sought. Today much of the growing and processing of the traditional 'Cameroon' variety actually takes place on the CAR side (Gamboula, Berbérati, Abba and elsewhere), and the name has settled into the trade less as a strict place of origin than as the name of a variety and style.",
      history: "Tobacco growing in Cameroon is said to have begun in the early 1900s, when the Dutch brought in Sumatran seed. Through German Kamerun (from 1884) and the Anglo-French partition after the First World War, the region passed under French rule, during which the French state monopoly SEITA managed the purchase and export of its tobacco almost exclusively. When France cut off tobacco exports from the region around 1993 and the industry's survival hung in doubt, Rick (Richard) Meerapfel — of the Meerapfel family, Brussels-based merchants who had long bought the region's leaf from SEITA — founded CETAC (Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains) together with local partners and kept production alive. Rick died suddenly in 2003, and his sons Jeremiah and Joshua carry the business on.",
      trivia: "Because Cameroon wrapper depends on a politically unstable growing region, supply is limited and its scarcity value high. Thanks to the efforts of the Meerapfel family, a handful of marques — Arturo Fuente and General Cigar among them — still use this leaf with its distinctive grains of oil (tooth). Substitute leaf raised from Cameroon seed in Ecuador and elsewhere has come into circulation in recent years, but the original African-grown leaf is more often judged the richer in flavour.",
      regions: ["Cameroon", "Central African Republic"],
      sections: [
        { h: "Climate, soil and wrapper aptitude in west-central Africa",
          body: "The wrapper the cigar trade calls 'Cameroon' grows in a belt near the equator stretching from eastern Cameroon into the neighbouring Central African Republic (CAR). English-language sources place the centre of cultivation on the plains north of Batouri, a town in eastern Cameroon, a region where moist wooded savanna spreads as the latitude rises. The soil is reported to be fertile, needing scarcely any fertiliser, with temperature and humidity well suited to tobacco.\n【A thin, delicate leaf】Leaf raised in this climate has a thin, delicate structure, and growing in open sunlight it carries a fine film of oils. It will not stand heavy fermentation, but that delicacy and sheen are exactly why it is prized as wrapper. After harvest the leaf is reportedly carried to processing plants on the CAR side — Gamboula, Berbérati, Abba — and this cross-border split between growing and processing pushes 'Cameroon' closer to the name of a style than of a place. Bear in mind, though, that information on Cameroon is scarce, and firm literature on the technical details of growing and curing is thin." },
        { h: "A tobacco history begun in the colonial era",
          body: "Tobacco growing here is said to have begun in the early 1900s, when the Dutch brought in seed of Sumatran origin. The region was then German Kamerun, established in 1884, and after the First World War it passed under divided French and British rule.\n【The SEITA monopoly】Under French rule, SEITA, the French government's state monopoly, bought Cameroon tobacco from a multitude of local farms and managed its export almost exclusively. But price manipulation and a cumbersome tender system drove away the cigar makers of Central America, and when France cut off tobacco exports from the region around 1993, this rare cigar leaf is said to have come close to vanishing into a footnote of history. It is hard to deny that the framework of colonial administration and state monopoly lay somewhere behind the instability of supply." },
        { h: "The Meerapfel family and CETAC — the architects of survival",
          body: "That Cameroon wrapper has survived to this day owes much to the efforts of the Meerapfel family, German-Jewish leaf merchants. The house traces its origin to 1876, when Meier Meerapfel supplied chewing tobacco to miners around Karlsruhe, and it is said to have been involved with tobacco origins across the world — Indonesia, the United States, Cuba, Central America.\n【The founding of CETAC】The Brussels-based M. Meerapfel & Söhne had long bought Cameroon leaf from SEITA; after the French withdrawal, Rick Meerapfel went into the politically unstable region and founded CETAC together with local partners, keeping production alive. After Rick's death his sons Jeremiah and Joshua took over the business. According to the trade press the operation involves some 3,000 farming households, and it is reported to be an important local employer (figures vary by source)." },
        { h: "Tooth, delicacy, and the difficulty of working the leaf",
          body: "The defining feature of Cameroon wrapper is its 'tooth' — the fine grains of oil that stand on the surface of the leaf. These are tiny pockets of oil arising in the tobacco, said to store flavour, and enthusiasts favour them. English-language sources say this tooth shows most strongly in the Cameroon variety grown chiefly in Cameroon and the CAR, some even calling it 'the toothiest of all varieties'.\n【Hard to handle】At the same time the leaf is thinner and more delicate than leaf from other origins, and it is known as a 'difficult' wrapper demanding a practised roller. Because it tolerates little further fermentation, it is rarely finished as a dark maduro and is usually brought to colorado — a rich brown. Its delicacy is also said to give an even burn that makes for a good smoke." },
        { h: "The flavour",
          body: "English-language sources describe the flavour of Cameroon wrapper as aromas suggesting cinnamon, baking spice, cedar, cacao and nuts, folded together with toast, a restrained buttery sweetness, and nuances of pepper and leather. The keynote overall is toasted, woody tones, against which a delicate sweetness and a peppery prickle sit in pleasing balance.\n【Middle strength, real complexity】The strength is broadly medium — rich without force — and that middle balance is held to be its virtue. So composed, it reads as complex and interesting to the seasoned enthusiast, yet approachable for the beginner. One view holds that the oils stored in the tooth lift the toast and sweetness as the cigar burns. For blenders it often plays the part of a 'secret weapon' that tunes the flavours of binder and filler, and it is known for balancing blends built on full-flavoured Nicaraguan leaf and the like. The taste of any individual cigar varies with its blend, though, so take all this as a general picture." },
        { h: "Unstable supply, scarcity and price",
          body: "Because Cameroon wrapper depends on a politically unstable corner of west-central Africa as its growing region, supply is chronically insecure and the leaf scarce. The English-language trade press records how a long supply crisis built up: years when drought badly damaged the crop in both Cameroon and the CAR (by one account a fall of about 95%), civil war in the CAR, and opaque dealing on both sides of the border.\n【Few users】The houses that kept using African wrapper on premium hand-made cigars were reportedly few — Arturo Fuente, General Cigar, León Jimenes — and even those companies are said to have gone through periods when they could not secure what they needed. Political, infrastructural and weather uncertainty has driven many makers away from the region, and the thinness of supply feeds directly into scarcity value and high prices. Figures and dates differ between sources, so firm statements are best avoided." },
        { h: "The major marques that use it",
          body: "The cigar that stands for Cameroon wrapper is Arturo Fuente's Hemingway series. Named after the novelist Ernest Hemingway and launched in the early 1980s as one of the house's early limited lines, it wears oil-rich African Cameroon leaf and consists mainly of figurados (tapered shapes). Made under Carlito Fuente, it is said to deliver toast, wood, nuts and caramel.\n【Partagas (the General Cigar version) and others】The non-Cuban Partagas began in 1974, when the Cifuentes family signed with General Cigar; it was made first in Jamaica, later in the Dominican Republic. Its long-standing formula pairs Dominican and Mexican filler and a Mexican binder with a Cameroon wrapper (the newer Partagas 1845 is a separate line using Ecuadorian Habano). Oliva Serie G, Nub Cameroon and H. Upmann Vintage Cameroon are among the others known for it." },
        { h: "Substitute origins — Cameroon seed grown in Ecuador and beyond",
          body: "Because the true Cameroon leaf is scarce and dear, attempts to raise the Cameroon 'variety' in other countries have been spreading. Ecuador is the best known, with examples grown in Honduras too, and La Aurora, Aladino and others are said to have had success with blends built on leaf of Cameroon-seed origin.\n【The difference from the homeland】English-language sources report, however, that growing Cameroon seed elsewhere cannot exactly reproduce the leaf of west-central Africa, and the African-grown leaf is more often judged the more flavourful and aromatic. The usual placing of Ecuadorian-grown Cameroon is 'a different thing — but an enjoyable experience in its own right'. With supply insecurity persisting, such substitute leaf seems likely to go on playing no small part, though judgements of it divide by maker and by individual cigar." }
      ]
    }
  };
  CIGAR_DATA.countries.forEach(function (c) {
    var e = EN[c.name_en];
    if (e) for (var k in e) c[k] = e[k];
  });
  if (CIGAR_DATA.philippinesDetail) {
    var P = {
      flavor: "Mild to medium on the whole, and often dressed in a light-coloured 'claro' wrapper. The style is described as gentle and approachable: floral aromas layered with nuts, cedar, light spice and toast, carrying a faintly sweet finish. Nicotine is on the light side. The distinctive point is dependence on foreign wrapper: with no large-scale wrapper production at home, makers generally import Indonesian Java or Sumatra leaf, US Connecticut leaf and the like, while Philippine leaf is said to serve mainly as filler.",
      regions: [
        "The Ilocos region (Ilocos Norte and Ilocos Sur, La Union and Abra provinces on the west coast of northern Luzon. The largest producing area, with over 60% of the nation's dried-leaf output, though its mainstay is Virginia leaf for cigarettes)",
        "The Cagayan Valley (the fertile basin of the Cagayan River. The reputed birthplace of Philippine cigar tobacco — Cuban seed is said to have been first planted here in 1592 — and the centre of native-variety growing for cigars)",
        "Isabela province (central Cagayan Valley. Source of the fine leaf 'Isabela burley', and the origin of the name of the venerable marque 'La Flor de la Isabela')",
        "Cagayan province (the north of the valley. It produces dark cigar leaf, chiefly from native varieties, and native strains such as Cimaba and Viscaya are said to survive there)"
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Spanish rule and the tobacco monopoly (1782–1882)",
          body: "The story of the Manila cigar is said to begin under Spanish rule on 1 March 1782 (the royal decree is dated February 1780), when the 53rd governor-general, José Basco y Vargas, launched the tobacco monopoly — the estanco. The aim was to make self-supporting a colony that leaned heavily on subsidies from Spain, building on a scheme conceived by the royal treasury official Francisco Leandro de Viana. The Cagayan Valley, the Ilocos region, Abra, La Union and Nueva Ecija (then part of Pampanga) were designated zones of compulsory cultivation, and quotas were imposed on the farmers. The entire harvest was bought up by the government at fixed prices, private growing and trading were forbidden, and the collected leaf was worked into cigars and shredded tobacco at government-run factories in Manila. The system certainly produced vast revenue for the treasury, but forced deliveries, low purchase prices and the corruption of supervising officials weighed heavily on the farmers, and it has also been pointed to as one cause of later Filipino discontent with Spain. Basco himself left the governorship in 1787, but the monopoly long outlived him." },
        { h: "Abolition of the monopoly and liberalisation",
          body: "After a century the monopoly gradually broke down under a combination of rampant smuggling, banditry, corruption in the monopoly administration and flattening revenues. That farmers in the Cagayan Valley, squeezed by harsh quotas and unfairly low purchase prices, endlessly diverted their harvests in secret is also said to have widened the cracks in the system. A royal decree of 1881 settled its abolition, and after the handover it effectively ended at the close of 1882 (some sources record 1883). The end of the monopoly opened manufacture and trade to everyone from large international companies down to the smallest private dealers, and farmers could thereafter plant as they judged best. In the early days of liberalisation, though, uneven quality became a problem — a complaint repeated after the free-trade laws of the American period that followed. To curb shoddy production and protect the trade's reputation as an exporter, raising quality became urgent, and in February 1916 a Tobacco Inspection Law was enacted to improve production methods and quality and to promote exports. The company that effectively inherited the industry after the monopoly, and carried the golden age that followed, was Tabacalera, taken up next." },
        { h: "The founding of Tabacalera and the golden age of the Manila cigar",
          body: "On 26 November 1881, Antonio López y López (the first Marquis of Comillas), a Spanish businessman who had made his fortune in shipping and trade in Cuba and Latin America, founded the Compañía General de Tabacos de Filipinas — 'Tabacalera' (La Tabacalera) — in Barcelona. A Spanish property-credit company, the Banque de Paris and a Dutch bank are reported to have been among the investors. The company took over the five government factories of the old monopoly, along with its plantations and distribution network, and dominated the Philippine leaf-tobacco industry almost as a monopoly. In the late 1880s (1885 by some accounts, 1887 by others) it built the great cigar factory La Flor de la Isabela at 851 Calle Isaac Peral (now United Nations Avenue) in Manila. At its height it employed more than 5,000 workers and was styled one of the largest and most modern cigar factories in the world of its day. The 'Manila cigar' exported from here had, by the end of the nineteenth century, built an international reputation ranked alongside Havana's. The line by the British writer Rudyard Kipling — 'Here is a mild Manila' — in his poem 'The Betrothed' (said to be collected in Departmental Ditties, 1886) is still quoted as a glimpse of the Manila cigar's standing at the time." },
        { h: "American tariff policy and the export boom",
          body: "When the Philippines passed under American rule after the Spanish–American and Philippine–American wars, the framework of trade with the United States came to shape the cigar industry. The Philippine Tariff Act of 1902 first lowered the rates, and the Payne–Aldrich Tariff Act of 1909 brought free trade with the US in the main products (cigars, it is said, under an annual quota). The Underwood Tariff Act of 1913 removed even that quota, and exports to America are reported to have grown strongly. Philippine cigars and leaf found markets in China, Japan, Britain, Spain and Australia as well as the United States, growing into the third export after sugar and abaca (Manila hemp). Growth was not unbroken, however: scholarship points to a structural depression in Manila's cigar manufacturing by the mid-1920s. The Tydings–McDuffie Act of 1934 began phasing out the tariff preferences, and with independence in 1946 the preferential free-trade regime came to an end." },
        { h: "The word 'Manila', and the cheroot",
          body: "In the nineteenth-century English-speaking world, 'Manila' was widely used as a common noun for Philippine cigars and for cheroots (cigars cut square at both ends, without a taper). The Oxford English Dictionary lists compounds such as 'Manila cheroot', 'Manila cigar' and 'Manila tobacco'. The word cheroot itself is said to derive from the Portuguese charuto, in turn from a Tamil word meaning 'a roll', and originally denoted cigars from southern India or Manila. Having no taper, the cheroot suited machine rolling and could be mass-produced cheaply, and in time some dictionaries came to record it as a byword for a cheap, coarse cigar. In the nineteenth century cheroots from Trichinopoly in southern India and elsewhere also poured into Europe and America through British territories, and being lumped together with these as 'cheap Oriental cigars' may be one reason a bargain-basement image attached itself to the name of Manila. Yet much of the contemporary primary record treats the Manila cigar rather as a fine product fit to rival Havana's, and firm evidence that 'Manila = cheap' was the dominant sense at the time is scant. Quality certainly varied widely with district and method, but the fairer reading is that the word drifted free of its origin and took on a life of its own." },
        { h: "The Second World War and post-war decline",
          body: "By its peak in the 1940s Tabacalera had grown into one of the Philippines' foremost conglomerates, employing about 27,000 people (beyond cigars it is said to have reached into shipping, sugar, spirits, copra, abaca, and power and insurance). But at the end of the Second World War, the Battle of Manila of 1945 — fought over the city's liberation from Japanese occupation — destroyed many of its facilities, and the industry took a devastating blow. The headwinds continued after the war. The cigarette became the world's dominant smoke and demand for the labour-intensive hand-made cigar itself withered, while the dark air-cured leaf in which the company excelled became an ever harder commodity against the light Virginia leaf pushed by American firms. Its near-monopoly at home crumbled rapidly from the 1970s, and between the 1950s and the 1990s Tabacalera sold off business after business. Its flagship cigar company La Flor de la Isabela was no exception, eventually passing into Filipino hands. The Manila cigar that had once rung around the world thus entered its long retreat." },
        { h: "The Philippine cigar industry today — two 'Tabacaleras'",
          body: "Cigarettes overwhelmingly dominate the Philippine tobacco industry today, and premium cigars remain a very small niche. Even so, the Tabacalera line lives on. La Flor de la Isabela, the Manila cigar house, made a fresh start as a Filipino company in the 1990s under the capital of the businessman Roberto Ongpin, and as 'Tabacalera Incorporada' styles itself the oldest working cigar factory in Asia, still rolling 'totalmente hecho a mano' — entirely by hand (production is reported at about 3.5 million cigars a year). Meanwhile 'Tabaqueria de Filipinas', founded in 1993 when the company's former general manager Gabriel Ripoll Jr. struck out on his own, is now held to be the country's largest cigar maker (about 15,000 cigars a day, some 60% of production exported, operating from a factory in Laguna province). The two fought over the trademark, and in 2009 (G.R. No. 161051) the Philippine Supreme Court in substance declined to rule against Tabaqueria, holding the risk of consumers confusing the two companies' products to be low. Tabacalera Incorporada has in recent years resumed sales to the United States through the American firm Daughters & Ryan." },
        { h: "Growing districts and varieties",
          body: "Philippine tobacco production is concentrated in northern Luzon. In the tallies of the Philippine Statistics Authority and the National Tobacco Administration (NTA), the Ilocos region (Ilocos Norte, Ilocos Sur, La Union, Abra) is the largest, with over 60% of dried-leaf production; by variety, Virginia is put at about 46% of the total, native varieties at about 25% and Burley at about 29% (NTA, 2019). Note, though, that these figures cover all tobacco including cigarette leaf — they are not statistics for cigars alone. Virginia is grown mainly in the four Ilocos provinces and Burley in Pangasinan, Tarlac and elsewhere, while the native varieties held to be the traditional raw material of the cigar are grown in many places but centred on the Cagayan Valley — Cagayan, Isabela, Nueva Vizcaya and Quirino. This basin, where Cuban seed is said to have been first planted in 1592, is sometimes likened in climate to Cuba's Vuelta Abajo, and is also known for the fine leaf called 'Isabela burley'. Tabacalera names 'Cimaba' and 'Viscaya' as the native strains used in its cigars, though this information rests chiefly on material from the company's own circle." },
        { h: "Blends and flavour — the dependence on imported wrapper",
          body: "Philippine cigars are on the whole mild, dressed in a light-coloured 'claro' wrapper, and described as gentle in taste: floral aromas, nuts, cedar and toast, with a faintly sweet finish. They are often introduced as light in nicotine and suited to the morning or early afternoon. The distinctive point is their dependence on foreign wrapper: with no large-scale wrapper production at home, the usual practice is said to be importing Indonesian Java or Sumatra leaf, US Connecticut leaf and the like. Indeed, Tabacalera's commemorative marque '1881' pairs an Indonesian Sumatra wrapper with a Philippine binder and Philippine-plus-Brazilian filler, while the published make-up of the top-of-the-range 'Don Juan Urquijo' is a Connecticut wrapper, an Isabela binder, and Brazilian, Dominican and Philippine filler. Tabaqueria's 'Independencia 1898' likewise fields a Java wrapper over an Isabela interior — Philippine leaf earns its keep chiefly as filler." },
        { h: "Availability in the Japanese market",
          body: "In Japan, too, Philippine cigars — 'Tabacalera' above all — count among the easier ones to find. Specialist shops handling the world's tobacco (Placer, Kagaya, Cigar Shop Kitahara, Sakimura Shoten and others) and various online stores carry the sizes — corona, half corona, robusto, panetela, pyramid — singly and by the box. Many sit in a modest price range, a single costing from a few hundred yen, and their easy handling, close to that of a dry cigar (a type kept without humidification), has made them a choice for beginners and for anyone wanting a light smoke in a short sitting. Not demanding strict humidity control in a humidor, they are said to be embraced as an everyday cigar for exactly that ease. That said, the premium hand-made marques of Tabaqueria and Tabacalera Incorporada get only limited distribution in Japan, and the truth is that the choice is smaller than for Cuban or Central American cigars. The mild, self-effacing style keeps a steady following as a light daytime or pre-dinner smoke, or as a palate-cleanser between stronger cigars." },
        { h: "Trivia and common misconceptions",
          body: "That several companies and brands bear the name 'Tabacalera' is the result of historical break-ups and reorganisations, and confusion is easy. The Tabacalera of Spain later passed into Altadis and then into Britain's Imperial Brands, and is said now to be a separate concern from the cigar side. The company actually still making cigars in the Philippines is the heir to the name, 'Tabacalera Incorporada'.\nA tale often circulates that Mark Twain praised the 'Manila cigar', but among his voluminous remarks on cigars none naming the Philippines can be confirmed, and the basis is thin.\nStories linking General Douglas MacArthur to Philippine cigars also appear, but the smoking article he is widely known to have favoured in the Philippines is the corncob pipe, and no source firmly tying him to cigars can be found.\nTabacalera Incorporada's '1881 Perique', meanwhile, is known as a rare experiment even by world standards: blending into a cigar the scarce Perique leaf harvested only in St. James Parish, Louisiana." }
      ]
    };
    for (var k in P) CIGAR_DATA.philippinesDetail[k] = P[k];
  }
})();
